& [SupportDelta] distinguishes the formed language from the proposed umbrella purpose
  + [complete] is canonical semantic-programming identity, compact core, bounded parser and compiler, signed package, exact query, CLI, MCP, service, faculty-cycle types, transitions, proofs, and live bounded provider seams
  + [formed_not_complete] is reflective VM, attention language, ontology import, general execution, neural compilation, database projections, FPGA profiles, cooperative junction, and compiler assistant
  + [absent] is stable general grammar, static semantics, general type and effect checker, module system, optimizer laws, backend ABI, conformance suite, multi-target fixture, neural builder, and migration tooling
  + [compatibility] the proposed purpose subsumes existing formed parts but must not reinterpret historical data as executable programs

& [RequirementDelta] is SMTC-001 through SMTC-030
  + [identity_delta] SOP becomes explicitly a multi-target language while Cantor remains one compiler and runtime implementation
  + [backend_delta] exact, neural, hybrid, attention, data, tool, workflow, hardware, and inspection targets receive separate profiles
  + [execution_delta] description, compilation, proposal, evaluation, authorization, and effect commitment become mandatory stages
  + [completion_delta] language completeness and ecosystem gaps become explicit rather than hidden by architectural breadth
  + [migration_delta] every existing SOP role requires inventory and compatibility classification before purpose revision

& [ReadinessDisposition] is umbrella SJS before purpose activation
  + [next] SMTC-SJS-01 inventories all current source roles, language forms, target profiles, runtime semantics, implementation evidence, and migration consequences
  + [first_candidate_after_authority] is one tiny program compiled to SOP-X, a bounded SOP-N blueprint, and SOP-H governed execution
  + [not_ready] is canonical purpose replacement, general executable-file default, neural printer claim, new compiler backend, or migration
